From the above discussion, when the droplet of solution is released in the air-liquid interface during self-assembly, the large numbers of OA- $\mathrm{.Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NCs form a large area of compact superlattice while MOF NPs do not have space to insert into the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NC superlattice plane. Thus, the MOF NPs sit on top of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ layer. The uniform bi-layered superstructure area can be as large as $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ as shown in Figure 2a. In order to study how the energetic interaction influences the self-assembly, the surface ligands of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were changed from OA to polystyrene (PS), a grafted polymer with higher molecular weight while retaining softness and strong ligand-ligand interactions, which leads to change of the energetic interaction in the diffusion rate definition based on Fick’s first law. Under the same selfassembly condition for assembling PS- Fe3O4 NCs with MOF NPs (details in SI), we observed that P ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}{\mathrm{NCs}}$ were separated by MOF NPs in the same plane, and the bigger MOF NPs
